{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>This shall be.</b> From here our Rabbis, of blessed memory, based [their teaching] that for the sin of evil speech plagues come upon [a person]. The metzora — they read it [as] motzi shem ra. And we also find four who were banished from the world on account of their tongues: Doeg and Ahitophel and Gehazi etc., as is stated in Cheilek, and also these have no portion in the World to Come. And it is written: ״אל תתן את פיך לחטיא את בשרך״ — in what? In tzara'at. And it is further written: ״מות וחיים ביד לשון״ — death, if he speaks evil speech; and life, if he engages in Torah. And the Torah is the remedy for evil speech, as it is said: ״מרפא לשון עץ חיים״ — for by engaging in Torah he refrains from evil speech. Evil speech is harsher than bloodshed, for the murderer kills one soul, but the speaker of evil speech kills three: the one who says it, the one who receives it, and the one about whom it is said. As we find with Doeg, that the people of Nov, the city of the priests, were killed — evil speech was spoken concerning them. And Saul, who received it, was killed for that sin, as is said: ״עמוד נא ומחתני כי אחזני השבץ״, and שבץ is nothing but the priestly garments, as is said: ״ושבצת הכתנת שש״. And Doeg, who said it — he was banished from this world and from the World to Come, as is said: ״גם אל יתצך לנצח״ etc. And we further find that evil speech is harsher than idolatry and forbidden relations and bloodshed, as is stated in Arachin." "<b>And he shall be brought to the priest.</b> They read in it והוא בא — he has no one to bring him, for everyone draws away and distances himself from him, as is said: ״בדד ישב מחוץ למחנה מושבו״."], :book :leviticus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "The comment is unvocalized throughout; read mutzi shem ra; בלע' resolved as בלשונם."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:08:17.947900354Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}